# 18.4: Using Bitcoin with MuSig2

There are two major Schnorr signature systems that have been built to
allow Schnorr multisigs. The first was FROST and the second is MuSig2.

## Understand MuSig2

MuSig2 is another Schnorr signing scheme that avoids the cancellation
attack. The big difference between it and FROST is that while FROST is
a threshold signing scheme (m of n), MuSig2 is merely a multisig
method (n of n). The only current methodology for using MuSig2 to
create a threshold signature is to create a MAST that lays out each of
the possibilities, as briefly mentioned in
[§19.3](19_3_Creating_a_Schnorr_Multisig.md).

FROST being able to natively offer threshold signatures is a big
advantage. However, MuSig2 also has an advantage of its own:
accountability. In FROST, you need insider information to know who
signed a multisig. In MuSig2, you can always guarantee every signator.

As with most tools, you should choose the proper one for your needs.

## Use MuSig2

TRY THREE

bitcoin-cli createwallet musig1

| {
|   "name": "musig1"
| }

bitcoin-cli createwallet musig2
{
  "name": "musig2"
}


musigaddress1=$(bitcoin-cli -rpcwallet=musig1 getnewaddress)
musigaddress2=$(bitcoin-cli -rpcwallet=musig2 getnewaddress)

musigpubkey1=$(bitcoin-cli -rpcwallet=musig1 -named getaddressinfo address=$musigaddress1 | jq -r '.pubkey')
musigpubkey2=$(bitcoin-cli -rpcwallet=musig2 -named getaddressinfo address=$musigaddress2 | jq -r '.pubkey')

musigdesc="tr(musig($musigpubkey1,$musigpubkey2))"
musigcs=$(bitcoin-cli getdescriptorinfo $musigdesc | jq -r '.checksum')
musigdescwithcs=$musigdesc#$musigcs

bitcoin-cli createwallet "musigwatchonly" true true
{
  "name": "musigwatchonly"
}

bitcoin-cli -rpcwallet=musigwatchonly importdescriptors '''[{ "desc": "'$musigdescwithcs'", "timestamp": "now" }]'''
[
  {
    "success": true
  }
]


